        }
      ],
      "id": "monisf4"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0ulj3fx",
      "content": "Public Finance refers to the study of government revenue, expenditure, public debt and financial administration. It deals with how the government manages its income and spending."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xy6v7lo",
      "content": "Main components: Public Revenue, Public Expenditure, Public Debt and Financial Administration."
    },
    {
      "id": "h9wzzf7",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. PUBLIC REVENUE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dglb3v6",
      "content": "Public Revenue is the income earned by the government."
    },
    {
      "id": "7076goc",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Examples</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Tax Revenue</p>",
            "<p>Income Tax, GST, Corporate Tax</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Non-Tax Revenue</p>",
            "<p>Fees, Fines, Dividends, Interest receipts</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Capital Receipts</p>",
            "<p>Borrowings, Disinvestment</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Grants</p>",
            "<p>Aid from foreign governments</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "16cqe0q",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. PUBLIC EXPENDITURE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "x37pe99",
      "content": "Public Expenditure refers to spending by government on development and welfare."
    },
    {
      "id": "0n8hqu4",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Examples</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Revenue Expenditure</p>",
            "<p>Salaries, Subsidies, Interest payments</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Capital Expenditure</p>",
            "<p>Infrastructure, Asset creation</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Plan Expenditure (earlier)</p>",
            "<p>Five-Year Plan spending</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Non-Plan Expenditure (earlier)</p>",
            "<p>Administrative expenses</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "gp0yggv",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. PUBLIC DEBT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "r223qxm",
      "content": "Public Debt refers to total borrowings of the government."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kbxwj2t",
      "content": "• Internal Debt – Borrowings within country (bonds, treasury bills)<br></br>• External Debt – Borrowings from foreign sources<br></br>"
    },
    {
      "id": "fiai4bf",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. FISCAL FEDERALISM IN INDIA"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9bg22ti",
      "content": "India follows federal structure of finance. Financial powers are divided between Centre and States under the Constitution."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "57uf6oi",
      "content": "Finance Commission (Article 280) recommends distribution of taxes between Centre and States."
    },
    {
      "id": "pd1ldyb",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. FINANCE COMMISSION"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Public finance is concerned with the income and expenditure of public authorities and with the adjustment of one to the other.",
      "id": "ui13vig"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nc2n007",
      "content": "Constituted every 5 years under Article 280 of the Constitution."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2g8adai",
      "content": "Functions:<br></br>• Recommend tax devolution formula<br></br>• Recommend grants-in-aid to states<br></br>"
    },
    {
      "id": "7kxz2jo",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. BUDGETARY PROCESS IN INDIA"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "511vus5",
      "content": "• Budget presented annually by Finance Minister.<br></br>• Passed by Parliament.<br></br>• Includes Revenue and Capital accounts.<br></br>"
    },
    {
      "id": "5ta45te",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "8. FISCAL POLICY TOOLS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hsslrap",
      "content": "• Taxation<br></br>• Government Expenditure<br></br>• Borrowing<br></br>• Subsidies"
